  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The General Services Administration has $23.4B in contract obligations for FY2026 to date. The agency recorded 1,109,938 awards, with an average award size of $21.1K. Obligations are down 1.6% year over year. Annual obligations rose from $17.7B in FY21 to a peak of $26.5B in FY24 and then moved to $23.8B in FY25 and $23.4B in FY26. Spending concentrates in Information Technology and engineering work, with Computer Systems Design Services at $4.9B and Engineering Services at $3.9B among the top categories. Automotive manufacturing and construction also appear among the top categories, with Automobile Manufacturing at $2.4B and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.8B. The top vendors by obligations include BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC at $2B and SCIENCE APPLICATIONS INTERNATIONAL CORPORATION at $1B. The FY2026 to date list also shows major awards to defense and systems contractors such as HII MISSION TECHNOLOGIES CORP at $873M and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $802M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What is driving GSA's FY2026 contract spending?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services and Engineering Services lead category obligations at $4.9B and $3.9B respectively. Automobile Manufacturing and building construction also account for large shares, with Automobile Manufacturing at $2.4B and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.8B. Several professional and technical services categories appear in the top ten, including Research and Development and Custom Computer Programming Services at $1.4B and $564M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who captures the largest shares of GSA contract obligations?",
        "body": "BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC is the top vendor at $2B and SCIENCE APPLICATIONS INTERNATIONAL CORPORATION follows at $1B. Defense and IT contractors populate the remainder of the top ten, including HII MISSION TECHNOLOGIES CORP at $873M, GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $802M, and CACI, INC. - FEDERAL at $797M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does General Services Administration spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The General Services Administration obligated $23.4B in contracts for FY2026 to date. The agency processed 1,109,938 awards with an average award size of $21.1K. Obligations are down 1.6% year over year. Fiscal-year trends show obligations of $17.7B in FY21, $20.6B in FY22, $23.9B in FY23, $26.5B in FY24, and $23.8B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does General Services Administration buy?",
      "a": "The General Services Administration buys a mix of IT, engineering, manufacturing, construction, and professional services. Top categories for FY2026 to date include Computer Systems Design Services at $4.9B, Engineering Services at $3.9B, Automobile Manufacturing at $2.4B, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $1.8B. The top ten categories also include R&D and specialized computer services such as Custom Computer Programming Services at $564M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are General Services Administration's largest contractors?",
